<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Russian steppe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Russia is a vast country spanning eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, which grew from the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. As a major power it fought repeatedly against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and later clashed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. Its steppe lands also formed part of the Mongol world and of the trade networks of Central Asian empires such as the Chagatai and Timurid states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russia.jpg" alt="Russia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter the Great · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Damghan and Murche-Khort and expelled them from Isfahan in 1729, liberating the Safavid capital after seven years of occupation; he pushed the Ottomans back in a series of western campaigns; and he induced <b>Russia</b> to return its Caspian conquests through the Treaties of Resht (1732) and Ganja (1735).</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the sixteenth century the homeland was divided between the Ottoman and Persian empires; Eastern Armenia passed to <b>Russia</b> by the treaties of Gulistan (1813) and Turkmenchay (1828), while Western Armenia stayed Ottoman.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By staying neutral but hostile to <b>Russia</b> in the Crimean War, Austria lost the friendship of the power that had rescued it in 1849, while France and Piedmont-Sardinia combined against it in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Christianity that the Byzantine state patronised and defined became the faith of <b>Russia</b>, Serbia, Bulgaria, Romania, Georgia and the Greek world — a “Byzantine commonwealth” that extended the empire's cultural reach far beyond its political frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The great conflict was with <b>Russia</b>, whose fortified lines advanced along the Terek and Kuban from the 1760s, beginning with the founding of Mozdok in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Visitors found the Hetmanate unusually literate: in the 1740s 866 of 1,099 settlements in seven regimental districts had primary schools, more per head than in <b>Russia</b> or Poland, and Paul of Aleppo wrote in 1654 that even wives and daughters could read.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
